    "case_id": "513",
    "case_summary": "A 78-year-old woman with a medical history of type 2 diabetes, chronic kidney disease, and hypertension presented with a 4-month history of sharp, pressure-like epigastric pain. The pain was associated with nausea, vomiting, acid reflux, and a 10-lb (4.5-kg) weight loss. She had no history of significant alcohol or substance use but was a former smoker.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were reported in the case.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Alanine aminotransferase: 16 U/L (10–50 U/L)    * Aspartate aminotransferase: 20 U/L (10–50 U/L)    * Alkaline phosphatase: 96 U/L (32–122 U/L)    * Lipase: 199 (13–60 U/L)- Imaging Studies:    * Ultrasound: Echogenic liver parenchyma with fatty infiltration, single hypoechoic lesion measuring 11 × 10 × 7 mm    Image Title: Doppler ultrasound of liver    Image Description: Positive Doppler flow within the subcapsular hepatic lesion    * MRI: 1-cm subcapsular enhancing lesion    Image Title: Arterial phase of contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging    Image Description: Shows enhancing subcapsular lesion- Pathological Findings:    * Liver biopsy showed islands of neoplastic cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm    * Immunostaining results:      - Positive for: human melanoma black-45, smooth muscle actin, and melanoma antigen recognized by T cells 1 (melan-A)      - Negative for: hepatocyte paraffin 1, human arginase 1, S100, SRY-related HMG-box transcription factor 10, inhibin, and glypican-3",
